WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.373
Walk up to a brick wall in a game. Head on, the bricks look deep. The mortar sits in real grooves.

00:00:06.723 --> 00:00:15.747
Now turn and look along the wall, almost edge on. Inside, the bricks still look carved. But the edge of the wall is a perfectly straight line.

00:00:16.097 --> 00:00:23.223
That wall is a flat rectangle, just two triangles. So where did the depth come from, and why does the edge give it away?

00:00:24.423 --> 00:00:28.839
Here are three ways to paint the same flat wall, under the same raking light.

00:00:29.189 --> 00:00:33.178
Flat. The brick picture is simply printed on, like a painted card.

00:00:33.528 --> 00:00:39.949
Bump. The lighting is faked from a height map, so the grooves get shading. But the bricks never shift as you move.

00:00:40.299 --> 00:00:48.555
Parallax occlusion mapping. The bricks slide against each other, and the front bricks hide the mortar behind them. That's what depth looks like.

00:00:49.755 --> 00:01:00.166
The idea is simple. Pretend the wall has a thin layer of depth behind its surface, described by a height map. White is the brick face, black is the deepest groove.

00:01:00.516 --> 00:01:07.065
When your eye ray hits the flat surface, don't stop there. Keep walking the ray into that imaginary layer.

00:01:07.415 --> 00:01:19.917
The more you look from the side, the farther the ray travels sideways before it lands. The sideways shift is the view direction along the wall, divided by how straight on you're looking, times a depth scale.

00:01:20.267 --> 00:01:24.171
Then read the brick colour where the ray really lands, not where it went in.

00:01:25.371 --> 00:01:32.603
To find that spot, the shader marches. It slices the depth into even layers and steps down one layer at a time.

00:01:32.953 --> 00:01:39.758
At each step it asks one question. Is the ray below the height map yet? The first yes means we've hit something.

00:01:40.108 --> 00:01:47.447
Then parallax occlusion mapping refines the hit, drawing a straight line between the last two steps to guess where they cross.

00:01:47.797 --> 00:01:56.927
On a test ridge, the refined guess lands almost exactly on the true crossing, while plain steps alone overshoot by about ten times as much.

00:01:58.127 --> 00:02:06.170
What if we take too few steps? With only a handful of layers, the ray jumps right over a narrow groove and hits the next ridge instead.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:12.195
On a real wall, that shows up as staircase bands, and grooves that swim as the camera moves.

00:02:12.545 --> 00:02:23.467
Here are four, eight, sixteen and thirty-two layers. Four is a mess. Thirty-two is clean. Against an exact test shape, the average error drops by more than forty times.

00:02:24.667 --> 00:02:29.254
One sanity check. Looking straight on, the ray doesn't shift sideways at all.

00:02:29.604 --> 00:02:36.473
So parallax and plain bump mapping should give exactly the same picture, and they do. Any depth here would be a bug.

00:02:37.673 --> 00:02:45.332
Now the edge. Here's a wall whose bricks are truly pushed out of the geometry, next to the parallax wall, seen from the side.

00:02:45.682 --> 00:02:52.509
The real bricks stick out past the outline. The parallax wall doesn't. Its outline is still the flat rectangle.

00:02:52.859 --> 00:02:58.640
Red marks every pixel where the two outlines disagree. That's more than half of the strip we measured.

00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:09.166
Parallax only changes which colour each pixel shows. It never adds pixels. The depth lives inside the rectangle, so the edge always tells the truth.

00:03:10.366 --> 00:03:15.699
Grooves should also be in shadow. That takes a second march, from the hit point toward the light.

00:03:16.049 --> 00:03:25.735
If anything blocks the way, the spot is shadowed. Even with a modest step count, it agrees with a march twice as fine on almost every pixel we tested.

00:03:26.935 --> 00:03:32.865
A few limits. This was a software renderer, so no claims about speed on real graphics cards.

00:03:33.215 --> 00:03:40.895
The refined hit is a guess between two steps, not an exact intersection. And a height map can't make overhangs or caves.

00:03:41.245 --> 00:03:47.091
The error numbers come from exact test shapes, not from the pictures. Every check in the run passed.

00:03:48.291 --> 00:03:54.051
Parallax occlusion mapping walks your eye ray into a height map, and shows the colour where it lands.

00:03:54.401 --> 00:03:59.286
It gives convincing depth inside the surface, for the cost of a few texture reads.

00:03:59.636 --> 00:04:06.932
But it can't change the outline. When the edge matters, you need real geometry, through displacement or tessellation.

00:04:07.282 --> 00:04:17.842
For further reading, there's Tatarchuk on parallax occlusion mapping, Kaneko and colleagues on parallax mapping, and McGuire and McGuire on steep parallax mapping.

